An iterator that forces promises or active bindings has the advantage of simplicity but has limited utility due to the uncontrolled side effects it'd cause. Also for consistency with the environment API, where retrieving a missing value causes an error, the callback probably shouldn't be passed `R_MissingArg`. It can't omit them either (would throw off bookkeeping), nor can it automatically throw an error.

I was imagining an environment iterator would call the user callback with an iterator context with fields (binding type and binding name) and accessors specific to the current binding type (e.g. delayed binding expression), or agnostic to the binding type (`value()` which may fail and cause side effects).


Something along these lines:

```
/* Public declaration (private one has iteration context) */
typedef struct {
    SEXP name;
    R_BindingType_t type;  // Can't be unbound here
} R_BindingIter;

/* Like `R_mapAttrib`, return NULL to continue iteration, non-null to stop */
SEXP R_mapEnvironment(SEXP env,
                      SEXP (*fn)(R_BindingIter *, void *),
                      void *data);

/* Type-specific accessors (no forcing) */
SEXP R_BindingIterActiveFunction(R_BindingIter *iter);
SEXP R_BindingIterForcedExpression(R_BindingIter *iter);
SEXP R_BindingIterDelayedExpression(R_BindingIter *iter);
SEXP R_BindingIterDelayedEnvironment(R_BindingIter *iter);

/* May force promises / invoke active bindings */
SEXP R_BindingIterValue(R_BindingIter *iter);
```
